 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Inference cost is significantly lower than NeRF. DONeRF requires approximately 4.35 MFLOP per pixel [S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Compact. Uses only two MLPs (approx. 4 MB total) without additional data structures or caching [S3].
-*   **Performance:** On an NVIDIA RTX 3090 at 800x800 resolution with 2 samples/ray, DONeRF renders in ~51.3 ms (~20 fps) [S3]. At 400x400, it renders in 22 ms per frame [S3].
-*   **Scaling:** Shading network cost scales nearly linearly with the number of samples (e.g., 34.9 ms for 2 samples, 270.4 ms for 16 samples) [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability, high quality (average PSNR 31.14 dB vs NeRF 30.52 dB), compact memory footprint, no need for explicit acceleration structures [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Not end-to-end trainable, struggles without high-quality depth supervision, poor performance on reflective/transparent surfaces, requires view cell definition [S2, S3].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time applications on consumer hardware, scenes with available ground truth depth (or synthetic scenes where depth can be extracted), large scenes where NeRF is too slow.
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with complex transparency/reflections, environments without depth supervision where training stability is critical, or when end-to-end differentiability is required.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference cost scales nearly linearly with the number of samples per ray. DONeRF requires approximately 4.35 MFLOP per pixel [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Compact. Uses only two MLPs (approx. 4 MB) without additional data structures or acceleration structures like voxel octrees [S3].
+*   **Performance:** Renders at 22 ms per frame on a single GPU (NVIDIA RTX 3090) at 400x400 pixels (approx. 20 fps) with 4 samples per ray [S3]. For 800x800 with 2 samples, total time is 51.3 ms (~20 fps) [S3].
+*   **Quality:** Achieves an average PSNR of 31.14 dB compared to NeRF's 30.52 dB [S3]. At 16 samples, DONeRF achieves up to 7 dB higher PSNR than NeRF [S3].
+*   **Speedup:** Inference costs reduced by up to 48x compared to NeRF when conditioning on available ground truth depth [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering capability, high quality with few samples, compact memory footprint, no need for explicit caching/acceleration structures.
+*   **Limitations:** Not end-to-end trainable, requires high-quality depth supervision (or pre-trained NeRF for depth extraction), struggles with reflective/transparent surfaces, view cell definition required for large scenes.
 
 ## Background
-DONeRF was introduced by [S3] (2021) as a method to bring compact neural representations closer to practical real-time rendering in games and VR. It addresses the prohibitive computational cost of NeRF by using a depth oracle network to guide sparse sampling. The method was developed at Graz University of Technology and Facebook Reality Labs [S3]. It is part of the broader effort to accelerate NeRF-like representations without sacrificing the compactness of MLP-based models [S3].
+DONeRF was introduced by [S3] to bring compact neural representations closer to practical real-time rendering applications such as games and virtual reality. It addresses the prohibitive computational cost of NeRF, which requires dozens of petaFLOPS for dense raymarching. The method was developed to enable interactive rendering (20 fps) on a single GPU while maintaining high image quality. It builds on the concept of depth oracles [S2] but introduces a specific dual-network training scheme, view cell unification, and space warping to achieve real-time performance without explicit acceleration structures.
 
